entries.notes:
The title at l.882 ('[Theorem 1 of \citet{bourgain2012spectral}]') and the statement at l.884 are the corrected text verbatim. Against EGF.BourgainGamburdTheorem1, clause by clause:
- d >= 2 is 2 <= d.
- g_1..g_k in SU(d) is g : Fin k -> SU d, with SU d = Matrix.specialUnitaryGroup.
- 'Algebraic entries' is IsAlgebraic Q of the complex entries. This is equivalent to being the root of a non-zero integer polynomial.
- 'Generate a dense subgroup' is Dense of Subgroup.closure.
- The operator f -> sum_j f(g_j .) + f(g_j^{-1} .) is heckeOp, on L^2 of the Haar probability suHaar.
- 'Spectrum on the orthogonal complement of the constants bounded above by some c < 2k' is the same statement as the Lean's Re<zf, f> <= c||f||^2 on mean-zero L^2 functions. The operator z is self-adjoint (for Haar measure, left translation by g^{-1} is the adjoint of left translation by g), and L^2_0 is invariant, so the supremum of the spectrum there equals the supremum of the quadratic form.

With the standard Q-bar (the complex algebraic numbers) the quotation is exact. But l.880 (v1 l.828, unchanged) defines Q-bar as the algebraic real numbers, and Theorem B.3 is its only use in v2. In the paper's own notation, then:
- every g_j has real entries, so g_j is in SU(d) intersect Mat(R) = SO(d);
- the generated group stays inside the closed proper subgroup SO(d), so the density hypothesis can never be met;
- the quoted theorem is vacuous. It is neither Bourgain-Gamburd's Theorem 1 nor the named Prop (finding 1).
This is not an overclaim, since the TeX is weaker than the Lean. But it defeats the entry's purpose ('quoted as its authors state it'; DEC-187's faithful named Props), and the batch creates it: v1's SO(d) reformulation applied Q-bar to real matrices, which fitted a real definition. Hence faithful = false until round 2.

l.887 is the corrected text verbatim, and each claim in it is anchored:
- the rotation of cosine 3/5 and its inverse: EGF.so2Witness;
- rational entries 3/5 and +-4/5: cos_angle35, sin_angle35, so2Witness_algebraic;
- dense: so2Witness_dense;
- the averaging operator Pf = (f o Phi_1 + f o Phi_2)/2 has eigenvalues in (1-eps, 1] on the mean-zero functions Re zeta^k, zeta = x_0 + i x_1: EGF.so2Witness_eigen, EGF.zeta;
- so the SO(2) analogue fails: EGF.not_spectralGapSphereReformulation_two, EGF.not_hasBGSpectralGap_sphere_two;
- 'Theorem 3.6 does not use a spectral gap': EGF.universality_sphere and EGF.universality_sphere_of_ergodic have no gap hypothesis.
The sentence's pointer '(Appendix~B.3)' sits inside B.3 itself (finding 8).

The refutation is answered: the printed SO(d) reformulation is no longer stated, and the quoted SU(d) theorem does not cover SO(2). added_hypotheses = [] is right, because d >= 2 is the source's own hypothesis, not one the correction adds. l.879-881 now contradict l.887 (finding 2).

